#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:2
msgid "Run Control File"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:3
msgid ""
"Xonsh allows you to customize your shell behavior with run control files, "
"called \"xonshrc\" files. These files are written either in the Xonsh "
"language or in Python and are executed exactly once at startup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:8
msgid "Xonsh RC Basics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:10
msgid "The control file usually contains:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:12
msgid ""
"Assignment statements setting `environment variables <envvars.html>`_. This "
"includes standard OS environment variables that affect other programs and "
"many that Xonsh uses for itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:13
msgid ""
"``xontrib`` commands to load selected add-ins (`xontribs <xontrib."
"html#loading-xontribs>`_)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:14
msgid "Xonsh function definitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:15
msgid ""
"`Alias definitions <aliases.html>`_, many of which invoke the above "
"functions with specified arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:17
msgid ""
"First of all, you need to know about the home directory ``~/.xonshrc`` "
"control file. This file is commonly used to put configurations for the user "
"interactive prompt and it is executed automatically only for interactive "
"Xonsh sessions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:19
msgid ""
"There are also a few places where Xonsh looks for run control files. These "
"files will be executed automatically in both interactive and non-interactive "
"modes, and you need to use the `$XONSH_INTERACTIVE <envvars.html#xonsh-"
"interactive>`_ and `$XONSH_LOGIN <envvars.html#xonsh-login>`_ environment "
"variables to determine what code you want to execute in each mode. Here is "
"the list of run control files and directories:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:21
msgid ""
"Cross-desktop group (XDG) compliant ``~/.config/xonsh/rc.xsh`` control file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:22
msgid ""
"The system-wide control file ``/etc/xonsh/xonshrc`` for Linux and macOS and "
"in ``%ALLUSERSPROFILE%\\xonsh\\xonshrc`` on Windows. It controls options "
"that are applied to all users of Xonsh on a given system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:23
msgid ""
"The home-based directory ``~/.config/xonsh/rc.d/`` and system ``/etc/xonsh/"
"rc.d/`` can contain ``.xsh`` or ``.py`` files. They will be executed at "
"startup in order. This allows for drop-in configuration where your "
"configuration can be split across scripts and common and local "
"configurations more easily separated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:25
msgid "In addition:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:27
msgid "Use ``xonsh --no-rc`` to prevent using control files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:28
msgid "Use ``xonsh --rc snail.xsh`` to run only a certain control file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:29
msgid ""
"Use ``xonsh -i script.xsh`` to run xonsh in interactive mode with loading "
"all possible control files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:30
msgid ""
"Use ``xonsh --rc rc1.xsh rc2.xsh -- script.xsh`` to run scripts with "
"multiple control files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:31
msgid ""
"You can create autoloadable `xontrib <xontrib.html#loading-xontribs>`_ as "
"alternative to run control file and reuse it as python package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:33
msgid ""
"The options set per user override settings in the system-wide control file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:35
msgid ""
"xonsh RC also lets you account for the operating system, so you can ship a "
"single file across systems and gate snippets by platform and execution mode "
"— see :ref:`Cross-platform xonsh RC <cross_platform_xonshrc>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:39
msgid ""
"Xonsh provides 2 wizards to create your own \"xonshrc\".  ``xonfig web`` "
"provides basic settings, and ``xonfig wizard`` steps you through all the "
"available options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:43
msgid "xonfig web"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:45
msgid ""
"This helps you choose a color theme, customized prompt and add-in packages "
"(\"xontribs\").  It initializes your personal run control file (usually at "
"``~/.xonshrc``).  To invoke it (from a xonsh prompt):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:48
msgid ""
"@ xonfig web\n"
"Web config started at 'http://localhost:8421'. Hit Ctrl+C to stop.\n"
"127.0.0.1 - - [23/Aug/2020 15:04:39] \"GET / HTTP/1.1\" 200 -"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:54
msgid ""
"This will open your default browser on a page served from a local server.  "
"You can exit the server by typing ``Ctrl+c`` at any time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:56
msgid "The page has:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:0
msgid "Colors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:58
msgid ""
"shows the  color themes built into Xonsh. Simply click on a sample to select "
"it.  Although color names are standardized across various terminal "
"applications, their actual appearance is not and do vary widely.  Seeing is "
"believing!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:0
msgid "Prompts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:61
msgid ""
"shows various sample prompts.  It is recommended to select one but to then "
"edit the ``xonshrc`` file to further refine your prompt."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:0
msgid "Xontribs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:63
msgid ""
"are community-contributed add-ins often used to enhance command completion "
"and line editing, but can affect any aspect of Xonsh behavior. Choose one or "
"more to suit your needs but note that they will require installation of "
"additional packages.  You can extend Xonsh by `writing your own xontrib "
"<xontrib.html>`_, and are invited/urged to do so!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:0
msgid "Save"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../xonshrc.rst:67
msgid ""
"Click to write the configuration choices to your ``~/.xonshrc``. This will "
"add a few tagged lines to your run control file, but will not overwrite it "
"completely, so you can run `xonfig web` at any time."
msgstr ""

